WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.180
After a meal, the sugar level in your blood rises.

2
00:00:07.240 --> 00:00:10.720
Small cells in your pancreas, called beta cells, then release insulin.

3
00:00:11.740 --> 00:00:13.240
Insulin is a protein hormone.

4
00:00:14.160 --> 00:00:17.340
The instructions for insulin are stored in the cell's nucleus.

5
00:00:18.140 --> 00:00:20.340
But the hormone leaves at the cell's outer edge.

6
00:00:21.180 --> 00:00:22.940
How does it get from one place to the other?

7
00:00:23.983 --> 00:00:25.243
Start in the nucleus.

8
00:00:26.163 --> 00:00:29.523
The nucleus holds most of the cell's DNA, its instructions.

9
00:00:30.523 --> 00:00:32.963
The gene for insulin is a stretch of DNA.

10
00:00:33.863 --> 00:00:36.463
The cell copies that gene into messenger RNA.

11
00:00:37.523 --> 00:00:40.043
Then the messenger RNA leaves the nucleus.

12
00:00:41.067 --> 00:00:43.767
The messenger RNA reaches a ribosome.

13
00:00:44.527 --> 00:00:45.967
A ribosome builds proteins.

14
00:00:46.847 --> 00:00:51.327
It reads the messenger RNA and links building blocks into a protein chain.

15
00:00:52.127 --> 00:00:55.427
The chain threads into the endoplasmic reticulum, or ER.

16
00:00:56.487 --> 00:00:57.727
Ribosomes dot the rough ER.

17
00:00:58.607 --> 00:00:59.907
There, the new protein folds.

18
00:01:00.807 --> 00:01:05.107
In a beta cell, this chain is a longer precursor, cut into insulin later.

19
00:01:06.150 --> 00:01:07.690
Next, a vesicle forms.

20
00:01:08.670 --> 00:01:12.050
A vesicle is a small bubble of membrane that carries materials.

21
00:01:13.010 --> 00:01:16.850
It buds off the ER and delivers the protein to the Golgi apparatus.

22
00:01:17.890 --> 00:01:20.950
The Golgi sorts the protein and packs it into new vesicles.

23
00:01:21.990 --> 00:01:26.930
Inside these vesicles, the precursor is cut into insulin, which is stored until it

24
00:01:26.930 --> 00:01:27.930
is needed.

25
00:01:28.333 --> 00:01:31.633
When blood sugar rises, mitochondria make more ATP.

26
00:01:32.513 --> 00:01:37.213
That rise sets off a calcium signal that makes more of these vesicles fuse with the

27
00:01:37.213 --> 00:01:41.613
cell membrane. Each vesicle then releases insulin outside the cell.

28
00:01:42.653 --> 00:01:45.413
Beta cells also release a smaller amount between meals.

29
00:01:46.353 --> 00:01:49.833
Building a protein chain and moving vesicles both use energy.

30
00:01:50.873 --> 00:01:54.733
Mitochondria release energy from sugar and store it in ATP.

31
00:01:54.733 --> 00:01:58.173
ATP carries energy in a form cell parts can use.

32
00:01:59.133 --> 00:02:01.393
So how do the parts of a cell work together?

33
00:02:02.313 --> 00:02:04.153
Each part hands its product to the next.

34
00:02:04.913 --> 00:02:06.573
The nucleus keeps the instructions.

35
00:02:07.713 --> 00:02:10.593
Ribosomes build the protein, and the ER folds it.

36
00:02:11.513 --> 00:02:14.093
Vesicles carry it, and the Golgi sorts and packs it.

37
00:02:14.833 --> 00:02:16.073
The membrane lets it out.

38
00:02:17.073 --> 00:02:19.533
Mitochondria supply the ATP along the way.

39
00:02:20.373 --> 00:02:23.773
A factory is a useful model, but no manager gives orders.
